WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.460
Film Courage: I know you said in another video
that when you were teaching at UCLA (and forgive

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.210
me if UCLA is on the quarter system or the
semester system…but…) okay each quarter

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:14.740
or semester you would see maybe two people
in the class that you felt were like gems

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.890
[i.e., especially talented writers) and the
others ones (although with time and proper

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:25.510
training they could become that)…I’m wondering…those
two people…those hypothetical students…would

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.120
they really know [that they were ultra-talented]?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.580
Could they see that they were different from
other writers?

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.849
Corey Mandell, Screenwriter/Teacher: Usually.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:31.849
Film Courage: Really?

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:33.350
How did they know?

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.620
How could you see it and they also see it
in themselves?

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:41.320
Corey: So…I was talking about this with
a manager the other day and I’m afraid I’m

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.970
going to over-answer your question so I apologize
ahead of time.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:50.570
So one of the key skill sets in addition to
creative integration is to build and write

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.210
compelling conflict.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:59.230
Compelling conflict is what takes great characters
and great dialogue and just makes it pop off

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:00.510
the page.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:06.480
It’s hard to explain but you have a visceral
experience when you read…look you read some

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.650
stuff and it’s just bad and then you read
some stuff and it’s got some great moments

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.960
and some great characters and dialogue but
nothing is really happening.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.100
Then you read some stuff and it’s good,
things are happening and it’s interesting.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.920
But then you read some stuff and it pops off
the page!

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:20.920
It just pops.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:21.920
You can tell.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.370
I was a studio reader and I could tell with
one page if someone knew what they were doing

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.050
if it popped off the page or not.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:30.340
Now if that one page was great, I don’t
know if they could write out a whole script

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:35.810
(I’d have to keep reading) but if that one
page didn’t pop I knew they couldn’t.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.750
And if I had a class of about 20 people there
are usually about 2 people and their writing

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.460
just sort of popped off the page more than
others.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:48.370
They didn’t know why necessarily but everyone
could tell and I think they could tell.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.360
They would often not say anything.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.850
It’s like in a writing group there is usually
just one person whose writing is just better

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.299
than everyone else’s or in an MFA problem.

00:01:56.299 --> 00:01:59.630
It’s just maybe people don’t know why
but it does.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.350
And what I’ve learned is it’s because
they’re writing compelling conflict and

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.950
I find that about 5% of the writers that come
to work with me naturally do that.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.700
I don’t have to teach them.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.410
It has that popping off the page.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:20.810
About 5% sort of do that and the other 90%
are anywhere from they need some work to they

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:21.810
are just bad at it.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.440
But you can teach people how to do it.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.590
So a great example is Neil Simon the playwright,
he has a book called ReWrites which is his

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.590
autobiography.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:34.420
And he talks about like year after year when
he was a younger man he’d write these plays

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.590
and he loved the characters and everyone thought
they were hysterically funny but there was

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:39.590
just something missing.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:40.590
They didn’t hold together.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.450
It just wasn’t interesting enough.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.830
And over time he’s like “I know something
is missing but I don’t know what it is.”

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.460
And I know a lot of writers who feel that
way.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.280
It’s a really terrible feeling.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.390
He eventually did the one thing he never wanted
to do, he went to his older brother who was

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.130
a really successful writer and his older brother
read his stuff and said you are not writing

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:03.410
compelling conflict and Neil Simon is like
“What are you talking about?

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:04.410
What is this?”

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:09.300
And his older brother taught him what it was
and then the light bulb went off.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.330
So then he said I would just structure my
compelling conflicts and then pour in all

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:19.500
of the character in comedy and that’s how
he did his career.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:27.191
David Mamet (and this is on my website or
you can look online) he did a memo about a

00:03:27.191 --> 00:03:32.930
decade ago or so to the writers of THE UNIT
(his TV show) and he basically said “You

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:37.930
will learn writing compelling conflict or
you will be in the blanking unemployment line

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.180
or the bread line.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.020
And there were a lot of expletives in this
memo but he was basically saying this is not

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.970
a natural skill set for most people.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.620
You will learn to do this or you will not
survive.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:56.060
And so I did not know it at the time and to
go back to your question actually (the truth

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:02.690
of my experience was) the 18 people whose
stuff wasn’t popping off the page, I wasn’t

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:03.940
able to get them there.

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:09.330
And so I like a lot of teachers (and I talk
to teachers at UCLA) and they will say this

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.330
privately.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:14.080
I don’t know if they’ll say this publicly
it’s like when you teach a class you are

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.839
teaching for that one or two person or people
who have what it takes, the rest don’t.

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:22.749
But look they have a dream, support them and
be encouraging.

00:04:22.749 --> 00:04:26.219
Don’t tell them they don’t have what it
takes, they’ll figure it out.

00:04:26.219 --> 00:04:30.979
And then when they figure it out they will
give up this dream and they will find the

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:33.990
dream that they have the talent for because
not everyone can be a world-class chef.

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.900
Not everyone can be a professional athlete.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.949
And so we teach for that one or two that have
what it takes and for the rest we don’t

00:04:40.949 --> 00:04:42.599
tell them that they don’t have what it takes.

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:45.589
We could be wrong but they will figure it
out.

00:04:45.589 --> 00:04:50.719
And that’s a real fixed-mindset way of approaching
this and one of the books that changed my

00:04:50.719 --> 00:04:55.400
life as a teacher is Mindset by Carol S. Dweck,
Ph.D. I make all of my students read this.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.779
It’s the only thing I make them read.

00:04:56.779 --> 00:05:00.370
And she talks about the “Fixed Mindset”
and the “Growth Mindset.”

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:07.370
And the Growth Mindset is okay these two people
who their stuff is popping off the page, why?

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.689
What skills, what talent do they have that
other people don’t have?

00:05:11.689 --> 00:05:17.189
The Growth Mindset says talent is repeatable
skillsets.

00:05:17.189 --> 00:05:22.559
And maybe you are born with them, maybe you
develop them through nurture and childhood

00:05:22.559 --> 00:05:24.810
or maybe you learn them as an adult.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.789
It doesn’t matter how you acquire them,
it’s just a skills set that is repeatable

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:31.479
as opposed to luck.

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:37.020
So repeatable skill set is you learn how to
drive a car and now you can always drive a

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.029
car or ride a bike.

00:05:38.029 --> 00:05:43.330
And so they have of the people that pop off
the page they have certain skill sets, certain

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.840
tools that other people don’t.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.590
You can figure out what those are (compelling
conflict) and then you can figure out how

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.680
to teach compelling conflict.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.439
Then you can teach those skill sets to other
people and they can learn that talent.

00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:05.259
So I had a writer who came to me (his name
is Gary) and he had a manager, he was always

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:09.539
taking meetings but he could never sell anything,
he could never get hired.

00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:14.680
He felt like he was that close to a career
for five years and at some point you’re

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.220
like “Okay, something is not here.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.220
Something is not working.”

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:24.999
So he came to me, a deeply intuitive writer,
he wrote the most amazing characters and dialogue.

00:06:24.999 --> 00:06:29.619
I didn’t…all I taught him on that front
was don’t change what you’re doing.

00:06:29.619 --> 00:06:34.250
You’re amazing, just keep doing that and
don’t let anyone interfere with that.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:39.800
But on the conceptual side you’ve got some
real weaknesses and him specifically was escalations,

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:44.710
how to keep making things get stronger…like
keep making things worse and worse in an organic

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:45.710
way.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:50.729
That was his…so he was an easy case in that
he had everything but one skill set.

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:53.339
He was really missing one skill set.

00:06:53.339 --> 00:06:58.849
So I have him those training exercises and
then unbeknownst to me when I was done with

00:06:58.849 --> 00:07:04.780
that class he spent the next three months
doing those training exercises everyday every

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.819
day for four hours.

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:07.069
I didn’t know that.

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:08.379
That’s a Growth Mindset guy.

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:14.560
That’s a guy who is like I want to become
an expert at this skillset that I’m naturally

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:15.560
am terrible at.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:21.189
And at the end of those three months, he was
as good at that as a natural conceptual writer

00:07:21.189 --> 00:07:22.189
is good at that.

00:07:22.189 --> 00:07:24.770
He was just as good at that as anyone.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:29.139
He now has a movie coming out…I think it’s
released in the fall [this video was filmed

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:33.309
in 2017] starring…what is her name?

00:07:33.309 --> 00:07:37.509
Anyway…not Reese Witherspoon but anyway
he has a movie that is coming out.

00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:42.129
He sold two agents, he’s sold two TV pilots
and he had everything but that one skillset.

00:07:42.129 --> 00:07:47.960
Now at the same time I’ve worked with writers
who are great at escalation and great at big

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:52.539
concepts but their characters and dialogue
is like…these are just puppets.

00:07:52.539 --> 00:07:56.830
And through the intuitive training they could
come out the other end where their characters

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:01.879
and dialogue are just as good as these people
who are naturally great.

00:08:01.879 --> 00:08:07.050
So the key is you have natural talents and
then there are talents that you don’t have

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.169
and you’re not that strong yet.

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:13.759
If you train the right way you can (if you’re
willing to put the time and effort in the

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:18.249
right way), you can end up being just as talented
as someone who is naturally that way and that’s

00:08:18.249 --> 00:08:19.639
the growth mindset.

00:08:19.639 --> 00:08:24.589
So for years I honestly and I’m embarrassed
so say this but for years I would teach a

00:08:24.589 --> 00:08:26.929
class and I’d come home and my wife would
go “How did it go?”

00:08:26.929 --> 00:08:29.379
And I’d say “These two people would have
careers.

00:08:29.379 --> 00:08:31.129
The rest of them, not so much.”

00:08:31.129 --> 00:08:36.000
And over time usually those two people would
go on to have careers and none of the others.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.440
And I’m like “Well, I teach for those
two people.”

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:41.900
I encourage everyone else because you never
know but probably they are going to find out

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.400
they don’t have what it takes.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.950
When I changed this approach and it took a
long time and all effort I remember I had

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:52.000
a class where there was only ten people, there
was one person who you could tell they had

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.000
what it takes.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:54.690
The other 9, no.

00:08:54.690 --> 00:09:02.510
And within 4 years, 1 of those 9 people dropped
out because of a long story so that left 8

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:06.010
and of that 8, 6 of them went on and got careers.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:15.060
And I wasn’t a smarter or more dynamic or
I wasn’t a better teacher than prior, I

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:20.160
just had a different teaching approach and
it was the approach that was radically different

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.020
(the whole creative integration skillset same
approach).

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:26.490
So it took me years and a lot of help from
other people.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.830
I didn’t just invent this all myself.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:34.960
But that is the really cool thing about talent
is that it’s repeatable skill sets and you

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:40.060
can learn these skill sets and you can develop
them if you know how to train yourself and

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:41.650
if you want to put in the time and energy.

